{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"useful-disposable-numbat","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Symptom\n\n`transition_ticket(fattish-plucky-bufflehead, \"in_progress\" -> \"closed\")` over MCP\nreturned `McpError: -32001: Request timed out` on three successive attempts. The\nticket stayed `in_progress` each time. The call runs the completion-verifier close\ngate inline, and that gate takes 9-11 minutes by `AGENTS.md`'s own published\nestimate, against an MCP client request deadline of roughly 60 seconds.\n\nThe timeout is not a verdict. The server keeps running, the caller learns nothing,\nand the natural response is to re-fire the same call -- a second billable gate pass.\nThis is precisely the failure `AGENTS.md` already describes for the direct gate\ntools, and the reason `review_plan_start` / `verify_completion_start` exist.\n\n## Why the existing workaround does not cover it\n\nPre-computing the attestation does NOT make the close fast. Measured on\n`62dc-535b-5d05-421d` at `origin/main` `f1fe7c6f587d3a57fcd4b49598485d7fd4101711`:\n\n1. `verify_completion_start` -> job\n   `1793725995508270393-89be127e921a4bf8b7acd1bbfaee7749`.\n2. `gate_status` -> `status: \"passed\"`, `verdict: \"PASS\"`,\n   `verified_at_sha: f1fe7c6f587...`, `signed_at: 1793726842234944794`.\n3. `transition_ticket` with that signed certificate in place -> **timed out again**.\n\nSo the async surface covers the gate op but not the operation that actually needs\nit. An agent that follows the documented guidance still cannot close a ticket over\nMCP without an ambiguous timeout.\n\n## Scope: which surfaces run a gate with no async counterpart\n\nIn `src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py`, `_start_gate_job` (:213-258) and `_spawn_gate_daemon`\n(:174-210) are already generic over `gate_type` -- the daemon machinery is not\nspecific to the two gates that use it. But:\n\n- `_register_gate_start_tools` (:261-342) registers exactly two starters:\n  `review_plan_start` (:265) and `verify_completion_start` (:304).\n- `_gate_sidecar_fields` (:162-171) hard-restricts to\n  `{\"plan_review\", \"verify_completion\"}`.\n\nSurfaces that run one of these gates inline and have NO async form:\n\n| Surface | Gate run inline | Async form |\n|---|---|---|\n| `transition_ticket` (`_mcp_tool_specs.py:649`) | completion-verifier on close; plan-review on claim-equivalent transitions (:657) | none |\n| `claim_ticket` (`_mcp_tool_specs.py:217`) | plan-review | none |\n| `review_code` (`_mcp_llm.py:354`) | code review | none |\n\n`review_code` is the clearest gap of the three: it is a long LLM gate op registered\nsynchronously alongside `review_plan` and `verify_completion`, which both got\nstarters, and it was simply not given one.\n\n## The invariant to establish\n\nEvery MCP surface that can run the plan-review, completion-verifier, or code-review\ngate must have an async counterpart returning a `{job_id, ..., status: \"running\"}`\nhandle pollable by `gate_status`, so no caller is ever forced into a request that\ncan outlive the client deadline. Today that invariant holds for 2 surfaces and\nfails for 3.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 -- `review_code_start` exists, returns a `{job_id, ticket_id, gate_type,\n      status: \"running\"}` handle in milliseconds, and its job is pollable through\n      `gate_status` to a terminal `passed`/`failed` with the review result.\n- [ ] AC2 -- `claim_ticket` and `transition_ticket` each have an async counterpart\n      (or an async mode) that starts the gate on the daemon, returns a handle\n      immediately, and applies the status change on gate success -- so the\n      state transition itself is never blocked on an inline gate run.\n- [ ] AC3 -- `gate_status` reports the new gate types, and `_gate_sidecar_fields`\n      no longer silently drops sidecar provenance for them.\n- [ ] AC4 -- a test drives each new starter through `gate_status` to a terminal\n      state, asserting the handle is returned before the gate completes (the\n      point of the change is the immediate return, so a test that only checks the\n      terminal verdict does not cover it).\n- [ ] AC5 -- a test asserts the invariant directly: every registered MCP tool that\n      can run one of the three gates has an async counterpart. This is the\n      regression guard that stops a fourth surface shipping without one.\n- [ ] AC6 -- `docs/mcp-reference.md` documents the new starters, and the\n      `AGENTS.md` guidance that currently names only `review_plan` /\n      `verify_completion` is widened to cover every gate-running surface.\n- [ ] AC7 -- de-dup protection applies to the new surfaces as it does to the\n      synchronous `review_plan` / `verify_completion`, so a re-fire after a\n      timeout attaches to the in-flight run instead of double-billing.\n\n## Provider neutrality\n\nNothing here is provider- or model-specific. The daemon machinery, the job handle,\nand the polling surface are all transport-level; they are indifferent to which\nprovider backs the gate.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Changing any gate's verdict logic, prompt, or criteria.\n- Raising the MCP client request deadline -- that is a client setting and would not\n  fix a 10-minute gate behind a 60-second deadline anyway.\n- The CLI surfaces, which are not subject to the MCP request deadline.","id":"cb1b-60f9-4832-4893","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","gates"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Gate-running MCP surfaces lack async counterparts, so closes time out"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793727338178088585,"uuid":"fa18703b-fb93-4792-b876-1a72961c1ab3"}